- dom-getnode-bypasses-element-cache: in `qjs-modules/lib/dom.js`, `GetNode()`
  (used by every `NodeList`/`HTMLCollection` traversal, and therefore by
  `Element.children`, `querySelector`, `querySelectorAll`, etc.) does not
  reuse the `Element.cache` WeakMap that `Document.createElement` uses.
  `Factory.for(owner)` returns a callable `fn`, and `GetNode` looks up the
  constructor via `factory(type)`, i.e. `fn(type)` = `fn[TypeName(type)]?.new`
  — the bare `.new` function. That function has no `.cache` property (the
  memoizing `cache` method lives on the sibling wrapper object `fn[name]`,
  not on `fn[name].new`), so `GetNode`'s `ctor.cache ? ctor.cache(...) :
  ctor(...)` always falls through to `ctor(raw, owner)`, constructing a new
  `Element` instance every time — even for the exact same underlying raw
  node. Found while building `qjs-nanovg/canvas2d.js`: a `<canvas>` element
  created via `document.createElement('canvas')` and later re-fetched via
  `document.getElementById('canvas')` or `document.querySelector('canvas')`
  compared unequal (`!==`) despite `Node.raw()` being identical for both,
  silently dropping any properties (event listeners, custom accessors, etc.)
  attached to the original instance.

    import { Factory } from 'dom';
    const factory = new Factory();
    const document = factory.Document.new({ tagName: '#document', attributes: {}, children: [] }, factory);
    const body = document.createElement('body');
    document.appendChild(body);
    const el = document.createElement('div');
    body.appendChild(el);
    el.myFlag = true;
    const found = [...body.children][0];
    console.log(found === el, found.myFlag); // expected: true true — actual: false undefined

- build-quickjs-forwards-cmake-system-name (cmake/BuildQuickJS.cmake:134, quickjs_configure_args): the nested
  quickjs configure gets -DCMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME=${CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME}. On Termux that is Android, and
  passing CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME on the command line makes CMake set CMAKE_CROSSCOMPILING=TRUE even
  though host and target are the same machine (quickjs-build/CMakeFiles/4.5.0/CMakeSystem.cmake
  says so). quickjs's CMakeLists.txt:727 then sets QJSC to the bare name `qjsc` (a host compiler
  expected on PATH), the generation of repl.c fails with "make[2]: qjsc: No such file or
  directory ... Error 127", and find_quickjs() stops with "Building quickjs failed". Found
  2026-11-15 on a fresh Termux (CMake 4.5.0, clang 21.1.8): the first configure without an
  installed quickjs fails in every project that has BuildQuickJS.cmake. Fix: forward
  CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME, CMAKE_SYSROOT and the toolchain file only when CMAKE_CROSSCOMPILING is true
  of this project; the quickjs side needs the matching fix (see ../TODO, host qjsc for a real
  cross build). Same code in the canonical BuildQuickJS.cmake of the quickjs-native-bindings
  skill and in the copies of the other qjs-* projects.

    # on Termux, no quickjs installed
    cmake -S . -B build/x
    # expected: quickjs is built, then the project configures
    # actual:   CMake Error at cmake/BuildQuickJS.cmake:189 (message): Building quickjs failed
    #           (see build/x/quickjs-build.log: make[2]: qjsc: No such file or directory)

- findopengl-cmp0072-not-set (CMakeLists.txt, find_package(OpenGL)): CMake 4.5 warns "Policy CMP0072
  is not set: FindOpenGL prefers GLVND by default when available" and on Termux, where both libGL
  and the GLVND libraries (libOpenGL, libGLX) exist, silently picks the legacy libGL. Set
  OpenGL_GL_PREFERENCE (LEGACY or GLVND) on purpose. Found 2026-11-15 on Termux.
